From: ash runtime investigation
Subject: [PATCH] process_events: unlink each node before dispatching it

Two heap bugs in the callback queue, both reproducible under MBHaxe's
multiplayer host/join and both ending in

    malloc: *** error for object 0x...: pointer being freed was not allocated

with the abort inside datachannel_process_events.

1. hl_dyn_call runs Haxe, and that Haxe calls back into process_events.
   An hl_mutex is recursive, so it does not keep the same thread out. The
   node was only unlinked *after* the dispatch, so the nested drain found
   the same list head and freed arg1.v.bytes a second time. Observed
   directly with a probe: "REENTRANT process_events depth=2" immediately
   followed by "DOUBLE FREE arg1 cb=onDescription depth=2".

2. Drained nodes were freed without ever clearing *_end, so the end
   pointer named freed memory. Between the consumer releasing the mutex on
   the last node and re-acquiring it to reset the pointer, a producer's
   *_alloc took its `end != NULL` branch and wrote end->next into the node
   just freed -- straight into malloc's metadata.

   The trailing `if (looped) { *_end = NULL; }` blocks were themselves a
   bug: the drain can leave early through `else break` with the list still
   populated, and clearing the end pointer there makes the next *_alloc
   overwrite the list head, orphaning every queued node while their
   semaphore permits stay outstanding.

Both are fixed by unlinking a node, and clearing the end pointer with it,
under the same lock that frees it. The consumer also re-reads the head
under that lock, since the value sampled before acquiring it can have been
replaced by a producer.

Note for anyone tempted to add a reentrancy guard instead: a depth counter
does not work here. A Haxe exception inside hl_dyn_call unwinds by longjmp
straight past process_events, so the counter is never decremented and every
later call returns early -- callbacks then queue forever and exactly one
ever dispatches. Unlinking before dispatch is what makes reentrancy safe.
---
--- a/cpp/src/datachannel.c	2026-08-30 00:24:51
+++ b/cpp/src/datachannel.c	2026-08-30 01:38:00
@@ -367,6 +367,15 @@
         datachannel_callback* res = datachannel_callbacks;
 
         hl_mutex_acquire(callback_result_mutex);
+        /* Unlink before dispatching. hl_dyn_call below runs Haxe, and that
+           Haxe calls back into process_events; an hl_mutex is recursive, so it
+           does not keep the same thread out. While this node is still the list
+           head the nested drain picks it up again and frees it a second time.
+           Clearing the end pointer here too keeps it from naming freed memory,
+           which the next *_alloc would write through. */
+        datachannel_callbacks = res->next;
+        if (datachannel_callbacks == NULL)
+            datachannel_callbacks_end = NULL;
         hl_rtc_datachannel* hldc = hl_rtc_make_datachannel(res->dc);
 
         vdynamic* args[2];
@@ -399,18 +408,11 @@
         }
 
         
-        datachannel_callbacks = datachannel_callbacks->next;
         free(res);
         hl_mutex_release(callback_result_mutex);
 
         looped = true;
     }
-    if (looped) 
-    {
-        hl_mutex_acquire(callback_result_mutex);
-        datachannel_callbacks_end = NULL;
-        hl_mutex_release(callback_result_mutex);
-    }
     looped = false;
     while (callback_results != NULL)
     {
@@ -419,6 +421,18 @@
         if (hl_semaphore_try_acquire(callback_result_semaphore, NULL))
         {
             hl_mutex_acquire(callback_result_mutex);
+            /* Re-read under the lock: `res` above was sampled without it, and
+               a producer can install a new head while this thread waits. */
+            res = callback_results;
+            if (res == NULL)
+            {
+                hl_mutex_release(callback_result_mutex);
+                break;
+            }
+            /* Unlink before dispatching, for the reason given above. */
+            callback_results = res->next;
+            if (callback_results == NULL)
+                callback_results_end = NULL;
             vdynamic* args[2];
             vdynamic arg1 = res->arg1;
             vdynamic arg2 = res->arg2;
@@ -456,7 +470,6 @@
                 }
             }
 
-            callback_results = callback_results->next;
             free(res);
             hl_mutex_release(callback_result_mutex);
 
@@ -464,12 +477,6 @@
         }
         else
             break;
-    }
-    if (looped)
-    {
-        hl_mutex_acquire(callback_result_mutex);
-        callback_results_end = NULL;
-        hl_mutex_release(callback_result_mutex);
     }
 }
 
